{"concepts_used":["GC-0128","GC-0115","GC-0116","GC-0117","GC-0102","GC-0104","GC-0097","GC-0124","GC-0113","GC-0135"],"decline_reason":"","declined":false,"citations":[{"ref":"TMM/Part52/2/2/1","quote":"Matters arising under the Act in which the Registrar is required to make a decision which will determine the rights of one of the parties involved relative to another party are referred to as “inter partes” matters."},{"ref":"TMM/Part52/2/2/1","quote":"Both of the interested parties have the opportunity to be heard before the decision is made, and the hearing is referred to as an inter partes hearing."},{"ref":"TMM/Part55/2#1","quote":"The legislation provides that the following actions may be opposed: Amendment of a trade mark application (s 65A) Registration of another party’s trade mark (s 52) Protection of an International Registration Designating Australia held by another party (reg 17A.33) Removal of a registered trade mark for non-use (s 96) Cessation of protection of a Protected International Trade Mark for non-use (reg 17A.48D) Grant of an extension of time of more than three months (s 224)."},{"ref":"TMM/Part46/2#1","quote":"When all the evidence periods have ended the parties are given an opportunity to be heard, in person or in writing, by a delegate of the Registrar on the merits of the opposition (section 54 and reg 17A.34M)."},{"ref":"TMM/Part52/3#1","quote":"In opposition matters, where the Registrar has allowed a reasonable time for all parties to indicate whether they wish to be heard, or where all parties indicate that they do not wish to be heard - per paragraph 21.15(5)(a) - the Registrar is not required to hold a hearing."},{"ref":"TMA1995/s203","quote":"The Registrar may not exercise a power under this Act in any way that adversely affects a person applying for the exercise of that power without first giving that person a reasonable opportunity of being heard."}],"answer":"**Who gets to be heard — Manual practice:** Both interested parties have an opportunity to be heard before the decision is made. The Manual describes a matter as **inter partes** where the Registrar’s decision determines one party’s rights relative to another party. [TMM/Part52/2/2/1]

**What proceedings are included — Manual description:** The Manual lists oppositions concerning:
- amendment of a trade mark application (s 65A);
- registration of another party’s trade mark (s 52);
- protection of another party’s International Registration Designating Australia (reg 17A.33);
- removal of a registered trade mark for non-use (s 96);
- cessation of protection of a protected international trade mark for non-use (reg 17A.48D); and
- grant of an extension of time of more than three months (s 224).
These legislative references are reported by the Manual; the corresponding Act and Regulations provisions are not supplied here. [TMM/Part55/2#1]

**Opportunity, rather than a compulsory hearing — Manual practice:** For opposition to registration or protection, the parties may be heard in person or in writing once the evidence periods have ended. The Manual also says a hearing need not be held where all parties decline to be heard, or a reasonable time has been allowed for them to indicate whether they wish to be heard. [TMM/Part46/2#1; TMM/Part52/3#1]

**Separate statutory safeguard — Act:** Section 203 requires a reasonable opportunity to be heard for a person applying for the exercise of a power before the Registrar exercises that power adversely to that person. This is a statutory entitlement, distinct from the Manual’s description of inter partes proceedings. [TMA1995/s203]"}
